<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sephardic Jews</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sephardim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sephardic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> are the Jewish communities descended from those of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal, expelled in 1492 and 1497. Many settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and from there in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guianas">Guianas</a>. They brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and trading expertise to colonies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> and Curaçao, where Mikvé Israel-Emanuel is the oldest surviving synagogue in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews.jpg" alt="Sephardic Jews" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alfred Dehodencq · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F%C3%AAte_juive_%C3%A0_T%C3%A9touan,_Alfred_Dehodencq,_1865.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Dutch Reformed Church was the public church, but Catholics, Mennonites, Lutherans and Jews could worship privately, and <b>Sephardic Jews</b> from Portugal settled in Amsterdam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch and Flemish refugees, Portuguese <b>Sephardic Jews</b> and, from 1567, the English Merchant Adventurers made Hamburg a centre of the cloth, sugar and Iberian trades.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>This pragmatic pluralism eased the integration of diverse skilled populations, notably the <b>Sephardic Jews</b> expelled from Spain in 1492, who brought commercial networks and technical expertise such as printing to Ottoman cities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>A notable <b>Sephardic Jewish</b> community, with its own self-governing settlement at Jodensavanne on the Suriname River, owned many plantations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Amsterdam welcomed the <b>Sephardic Jews</b> and granted them all privileges except citizenship, but its tolerance served the city’s convenience: poor Ashkenazi Jews from Eastern Europe were far more carefully vetted, and those who came to depend on the city were encouraged to move on.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sephardim</b> and British synagogues sing it at the close of the Shabbat and festival morning service, many communities at the end of Mussaf, the German rite at the start of daily morning prayers, and Ashkenazim sometimes in place of Yigdal at the close of the evening service.</blockquote><a href="/songs/adon-olam">Adon Olam</a></li></ul></section>
